<article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article" dtd-version="1.3"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Anaesth</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Br J Anaesth</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>BJA: British Journal of Anaesthesia</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0912</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1471-6771</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Elsevier</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">38762396</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">11213984</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">S0007-0912(24)00219-8</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.bja.2024.04.019</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regional Anaesthesia</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Ultrasound-guided suprainguinal fascia iliaca compartment block and early postoperative analgesia after total hip arthroplasty: a randomised controlled trial</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au1"><name><surname>Safa</surname><given-names>Ben</given-names></name><email>ben.safa@sunnybrook.ca</email><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">1</xref><xref rid="cor1" ref-type="corresp">&#x0204e;</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au2"><name><surname>Trinh</surname><given-names>Hawn</given-names></name><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au3"><name><surname>Lansdown</surname><given-names>Andrew</given-names></name><xref rid="aff3" ref-type="aff">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au4"><name><surname>McHardy</surname><given-names>Paul G.</given-names></name><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au5"><name><surname>Gollish</surname><given-names>Jeffrey</given-names></name><xref rid="aff4" ref-type="aff">4</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au6"><name><surname>Kiss</surname><given-names>Alex</given-names></name><xref rid="aff5" ref-type="aff">5</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au7"><name><surname>Kaustov</surname><given-names>Lilia</given-names></name><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au8"><name><surname>Choi</surname><given-names>Stephen</given-names></name><email>stephen.choi@sunnybrook.ca</email><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">1</xref><xref rid="cor1" ref-type="corresp">&#x0204e;</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>Department of Anesthesia, Sunnybrook Health Sciences Centre, University of Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada</aff><aff id="aff2"><label>2</label>University of New South Wales, Sydney, NSW, Australia</aff><aff id="aff3"><label>3</label>Royal Prince Alfred Hospital, Sydney, NSW, Australia</aff><aff id="aff4"><label>4</label>Division of Orthopaedic Surgery, Department of Surgery, Sunnybrook Health Sciences Centre, University of Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada</aff><aff id="aff5"><label>5</label>Institute for Evaluative Clinical Sciences, University of Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada</aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="cor1"><label>&#x0204e;</label>Corresponding authors. <email>ben.safa@sunnybrook.ca</email><email>stephen.choi@sunnybrook.ca</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>18</day><month>5</month><year>2024</year></pub-date><!-- PMC Release delay is 0 months and 0 days and was based on <pub-date
						pub-type="epub">.--><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>7</month><year>2024</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>18</day><month>5</month><year>2024</year></pub-date><volume>133</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>146</fpage><lpage>151</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>23</day><month>1</month><year>2024</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>3</day><month>4</month><year>2024</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; 2024 The Author(s)</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2024</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).</license-p></license></permissions><abstract id="abs0010"><sec><title>Background</title><p>Hip replacement surgery can be painful; postoperative analgesia is crucial for comfort and to facilitate recovery. Regional anaesthesia can reduce pain and postoperative opioid requirements. The role of ultrasound-guided suprainguinal fascia iliaca block for analgesia after elective total hip arthroplasty is not well defined. This randomised trial evaluated its analgesic efficacy.</p></sec><sec><title>Methods</title><p>Consenting participants (134) scheduled for elective primary total hip arthroplasty under spinal anaesthesia were randomly allocated to receive ultrasound-guided fascia iliaca block with ropivacaine 0.5% or sham block with saline. The primary outcome was opioid consumption in the first 24 h after surgery. Additional outcomes included pain scores at 4, 8, 12, and 16 h, opioid-related side-effects (nausea, vomiting, pruritis), ability to perform physiotherapy on the first postoperative day, and physiotherapist-assessed quadriceps weakness.</p></sec><sec><title>Results</title><p>There were no significant differences in 24-h opioid consumption (block <italic>vs</italic> sham block, mean difference&#x000a0;&#x02212;3.2 mg oral morphine equivalent, 95% confidence interval&#x000a0;&#x02212;15.3 to 8.1 mg oral morphine equivalent, <italic>P</italic>=0.55) or any other prespecified outcomes.</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusions</title><p>In patients undergoing primary total hip arthroplasty, ultrasound-guided suprainguinal fascia iliaca block with ropivacaine did not confer a significant opioid-sparing effect compared with sham block. There were no differences in other secondary outcomes including pain scores, opioid-related side-effects, or ability to perform physiotherapy on the first postoperative day.</p></sec><sec><title>Clinical trial registration</title><p>www.clinicaltrials.gov (NCT03069183).</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group id="kwrds0010"><title>Keywords</title><kwd>fascia iliaca block</kwd><kwd>hip arthroplasty</kwd><kwd>regional anaesthesia</kwd><kwd>suprainguinal</kwd><kwd>ultrasound-guided</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta><notes><p id="misc0010">Handling Editor: Hugh C Hemmings Jr</p></notes></front><body><p id="p0010">
<boxed-text id="dtbox1"><caption><title>Editor's key points</title></caption><p id="p0015">
<list list-type="simple" id="ulist0010"><list-item id="u0010"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0020">The optimal analgesic strategy for total hip arthroplasty, which could impact the risk of perioperative adverse events and facilitate enhanced recovery, remains controversial.</p></list-item><list-item id="u0015"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0025">The analgesic efficacy of ultrasound-guided suprainguinal fascia iliaca block for analgesia after elective total hip arthroplasty was examined in this single-centre randomised trial.</p></list-item><list-item id="u0020"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0030">Addition of ultrasound-guided suprainguinal fascia iliaca block with ropivacaine to a robust multimodal analgesic regimen was not superior to sham block in reducing opioid consumption.</p></list-item><list-item id="u0025"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0035">Additional investigation is required to determine the role of the suprainguinal fascia iliaca block in ambulatory total hip arthroplasty.</p></list-item></list>
</p></boxed-text>
</p><p id="p0040">Total hip arthroplasty (THA) is a major surgical procedure associated with significant pain, commonly performed in older patients with co-morbidities.<xref rid="bib1" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref> The optimal analgesic strategy remains controversial but could play a significant role in reducing the risk of perioperative adverse events and in facilitating enhanced recovery, thereby accelerating hospital discharge and functional recovery. Peripheral nerve blocks are increasingly used to provide opioid-sparing analgesia, reduce complications, and improve patient satisfaction.<xref rid="bib2" ref-type="bibr"><sup>2</sup></xref></p><p id="p0045">Sensory innervation of the hip is complex and from multiple sources. The hip capsule receives innervation from multiple nerves of the lumbosacral plexus. Specifically, branches from the femoral (FN), obturator (ON), and accessory obturator (AON) nerves provide innervation to the anterior hip capsule, whereas the nerve to quadratus femoris (derived from the sacral plexus (L4, L5, S1 anterior divisions) reliably innervates the posterior aspect. There are also contributions from the accessory femoral (AFN), superior gluteal, accessory superior gluteal, inferior gluteal, sciatic, and posterior femoral cutaneous nerves, although with less consistency.<xref rid="bib3" ref-type="bibr"><sup>3</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref> A variety of regional anaesthesia techniques have been used to reduce postoperative pain after THA. However, the choice of an optimal form of analgesia, including regional block for elective THA, is not well defined.</p><p id="p0050">The posterior approach to the lumbar plexus is effective but technically challenging and is associated with significant complications (e.g. haematoma, block failure). Initially described by Hebbard and colleagues<xref rid="bib5" ref-type="bibr"><sup>5</sup></xref> and later modified by Desmet and colleagues,<xref rid="bib6" ref-type="bibr"><sup>6</sup></xref> ultrasound-guided suprainguinal fascia iliaca block (US-SIFIB) has emerged as a safer, &#x02018;anterior&#x02019; approach to the lumbar plexus and an analgesic option for THA. For US-SIFIB, a large volume local anaesthetic (&#x0223c;40 ml) injection into the fascial plane between the iliacus muscle and the fascia iliaca above the inguinal ligament facilitates greater cephalad spread. This is effectively an anterior approach to the lumbar plexus and increases the probability of blocking the FN, ON, AON, and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves to provide effective analgesia.</p><p id="p0055">Previous studies have demonstrated reduced opioid use and pain scores with US-SIFIB. However, the efficacy of US-SIFIB for analgesia after THA remains controversial because of the limited number, heterogeneity, and quality of published studies.<xref rid="bib7" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref> We conducted a randomised, blinded, placebo-controlled trial examining the early analgesic efficacy of US-SIFIB after THA under spinal anaesthesia. We hypothesised that US-SIFIB would provide superior analgesic outcomes by reducing opioid consumption in the first 24 h after THA.</p><sec id="sec1"><title>Methods</title><p id="p0060">This study was approved by the Research Ethics Board at Sunnybrook Health Sciences Centre (SHSC) in April 2017 (REB ID# 3222) and registered with <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.clinicaltrials.gov" id="intref0010">www.clinicaltrials.gov</ext-link> (March 3, 2017 NCT03069183). It was designed as a prospective, single-centre, randomised, placebo-controlled, parallel group trial at SHSC, a tertiary care academic health science centre affiliated with the University of Toronto. All participants provided written informed consent before enrolment.</p><p id="p0065">Eligible participants were those scheduled to undergo elective primary unilateral THA (lateral surgical approach) aged 18&#x02013;80 yr with ASA physical status 1&#x02013;3. Patients were excluded if they did not provide consent, had BMI &#x0003e;45 kg m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup>, pre-existing medical conditions precluding spinal anaesthesia or regional blocks, an allergy or contraindication to local anaesthetics or components of the multimodal analgesic regimen, prior surgery in the inguinal or suprainguinal area, were pregnant, or were on chronic opioid therapy (&#x0003e;30 mg oral morphine equivalent [OME] daily &#x0003e;30 days). Trained research personnel recruited patients from the preoperative anaesthesia clinic.</p><p id="p0070">After providing informed consent, participants were sequentially randomised (1:1) in blocks of four using a computer-generated sequence (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.sealeenvelope.com" id="intref0015">www.sealeenvelope.com</ext-link>). Allocation concealment was maintained with sequentially numbered sealed opaque envelopes. Participants were randomised into one of two treatment arms: US-SIFIB with ropivacaine 0.5% (block group) or with 0.9% NS (sham block group). The randomisation list was kept in a secure locked location inaccessible to investigators or study personnel. Data were analysed as group &#x02018;A&#x02019; <italic>vs</italic> group &#x02018;B&#x02019; with group allocation revealed only after analysis was completed.</p><p id="p0075">On the day of surgery all participants received oral paracetamol 1 g p.o., celecoxib 400 mg p.o., and gabapentin 400 mg p.o. 1&#x02013;2 h before the operation and were transferred to a designated regional anaesthesia area. An i.v. infusion of lactated Ringer's solution was initiated, and noninvasive arterial pressure, ECG, and pulse oximetry were initiated. After midazolam (0.03 mg kg<sup>&#x02212;1</sup> i.v.) for anxiolysis, US-SIFIB was performed in the supine position using a 38 mm broadband 13-6 MHz linear array ultrasound probe positioned in a parasagittal orientation, medial to the anterosuperior iliac spine. The &#x02018;bow-tie&#x02019; sign was obtained as described.<xref rid="bib5" ref-type="bibr"><sup>5</sup></xref> Under aseptic conditions, a 22-G 80 mm Stimuplex needle (B Braun, Melsungen, Germany) was inserted in plane caudad-to-cephalad and advanced 1&#x02013;2 cm superior to the inguinal ligament into the suprainguinal subfascial iliaca compartment. The block needle tip was positioned between the internal oblique and iliacus muscles underneath the fascia iliaca; after negative aspiration, a volume of 40 ml of the study solution was injected. The volume was reduced to 30 ml in those weighing &#x0003c;60 kg. All blocks were performed by experienced fellowship-trained regional anaesthesiologists or by trainees under direct supervision.</p><p id="p0080">The study solution was prepared by trained anaesthesia assistants in syringes of identical appearance and labelled as &#x02018;study injection&#x02019;. Anaesthesia assistants were aware of group allocation but had no further role in study procedures or assessments. Participants, anaesthesiologists, surgeons, nurses, physical therapists, and data collectors and analysts were blinded to group allocation.</p><p id="p0085">All participants received spinal anaesthesia with 2.8&#x02013;3 ml (discretion of blinded anaesthesiologist based on participant height) of isobaric bupivacaine 0.5% and fentanyl 15 &#x003bc;g. Surgery was performed using the lateral approach. Intraoperative sedation was maintained with propofol (25&#x02013;100 &#x003bc;g kg<sup>&#x02212;1</sup> min <sup>&#x02212;1</sup>) titrated to moderate sedation until the end of surgery. All participants received i.v. dexamethasone (8 mg) and ondansetron (4 mg) for nausea prophylaxis.</p><p id="p0090">The postoperative analgesic regimen included paracetamol 1 g p.o. every 6 h, hydromorphone controlled release 3 mg p.o. every 8 h, and celecoxib 200 mg p.o. every 12 h. I.V. patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) (hydromorphone 0.2 mg; lockout interval of 5 min) was initiated in the postanaesthesia care unit (PACU). At the time of PCA initiation in the PACU (0 h) and every 4 h for the next 24 h, pain intensity at rest and with mobilisation was recorded using a numerical rating pain score (NRS-11 0=no pain, 10=worst pain). Oral hydromorphone 1&#x02013;2 mg every 2 h as required was prescribed upon discontinuation of PCA after 24 h.</p><sec id="sec1.1"><title>Outcomes</title><p id="p0095">The primary outcome was cumulative opioid consumption the first 24 h after THA. This was determined from the PCA pump data and nursing medication administration records. Doses were converted to OME according to the general monograph for opioids in the Canadian Pharmacists' Association Compendium of Pharmaceuticals and Specialties (oral hydromorphone:oral morphine=1:5, oral oxycodone:oral morphine=1:2, i.v.:oral morphine=1:2).</p><p id="p0100">Secondary outcomes included: pain scores (NRS-11) on arrival to PACU and every 4 h thereafter for 24 h at rest and during mobilisation; opioid-related side-effects (nausea, vomiting [grading: 0=absence, 1=mild, 2= moderate, 3=severe]), pruritis, dizziness, and respiratory depression); ability to perform physiotherapy at 24 h (1=able, 2=able with support, 3=unable), the reason for requiring support or inability to participate (1=medical status, 2=nausea or vomiting, 3=quadriceps weakness, 4=pain, 5=other); presence or absence of quadriceps weakness and degree of quadriceps weakness if present. This was determined by subjective judgment of blinded physiotherapists during routine postoperative physiotherapy sessions. Two outcomes were dichotomised (yes/no) to facilitate analysis: (a) nausea or vomiting, and (b) ability to participate in physiotherapy.</p></sec><sec id="sec1.2"><title>Sample size</title><p id="p0105">The sample size was informed by our historical institutional data. A previous study published from our centre indicated a 24-h PCA morphine consumption of 34.4 mg (20.9).<xref rid="bib8" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref> We chose a 30% reduction in opioid consumption as clinically significant (i.e. US-SIFIB reduces i.v. morphine use by 10.2 mg&#x02013;24.2 mg). With a Type I error at 0.05 and Type II error at 0.2 (power 80%), a sample size of 132 participants (66 per arm) was required.</p></sec><sec id="sec1.3"><title>Statistical analysis</title><p id="p0110">Patient characteristic data are summarised and expressed using appropriate measures of central tendency and dispersion or counts and percentages for categorical data. The primary outcome (24-h opioid consumption) was compared between groups using the unpaired <italic>t</italic>-test. For secondary outcomes, NRS over time was assessed with analysis of variance (<sc>anova</sc>). In the event <sc>anova</sc> was statistically significant, the Tukey test was planned for pairwise comparisons. Categorical outcomes were compared with the &#x003c7;<sup>2</sup> test (Fisher's exact test for low expected cell counts). Because the trial was powered to 24-h opioid consumption and secondary outcomes were considered exploratory (i.e. sample size underpowered to detect differences), corrections for multiple comparisons were not utilised. All data were analysed on an intention-to-treat basis and inferential testing was two-sided.</p></sec></sec><sec id="sec2"><title>Results</title><p id="p0115">The CONSORT flow diagram details the number of individuals approached, recruited, and randomised (<xref rid="fig1" ref-type="fig">Fig.&#x000a0;1</xref>). Among 692 eligible patients approached, 399 declined to participate, 159 had one or more exclusion criteria, and 134 were enrolled and consented between August 31, 2018 and April 27, 2022. Among those providing consent, one withdrew from the study after randomisation. Of the remaining 133 participants, 66 were randomised to the block group and 67 to the sham block group (<xref rid="fig1" ref-type="fig">Fig.&#x000a0;1</xref>).<fig id="fig1"><label>Fig 1</label><caption><p>CONSORT flow diagram of approached, consented, and randomised participants.</p></caption><alt-text id="alttext0015">Fig 1</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr1"/></fig></p><p id="p0120">There were three protocol deviations in the block group. One subject did not receive celecoxib after operation, one was not given PCA and only received oral opioids, and one did not receive dexamethasone intraoperatively. There were two protocol deviations in the sham block group. One received ketamine intraoperatively, and one did not receive dexamethasone intraoperatively. Data for all were collected and analysed in the group to which they were randomised.</p><p id="p0125">Patient characteristics are presented in <xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">Table&#x000a0;1</xref> with no gross differences between groups.<table-wrap position="float" id="tbl1"><label>Table&#x000a0;1</label><caption><p>Study population characteristics. ASA, American Society of Anesthesiologists; BMI, body mass index; NS, normal saline; US-SIFIB, ultrasound-guided suprainguinal fascia iliaca compartment block.</p></caption><alt-text id="alttext0025">Table&#x000a0;1</alt-text><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th/><th>US-SIFIB (block <italic>n</italic>=66)</th><th>0.9% NS (sham block <italic>n</italic>=67)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Age (yr)</td><td>63.9 (10.9)</td><td>65.2 (9.5)</td></tr><tr><td>Sex (M/F)</td><td>27/39</td><td>35/32</td></tr><tr><td>ASA physical status 1/2/3</td><td>9/37/20</td><td>4/39/24</td></tr><tr><td>BMI (kg m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup>)</td><td>28.7 (5.2)</td><td>29.3 (5.0)</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></p><p id="p0130">We did not observe significant differences between groups (block <italic>vs</italic> sham block) in 24-h opioid consumption (<xref rid="fig2" ref-type="fig">Fig.&#x000a0;2</xref>). Mean OME opioid consumption at 24 h was 63.4 mg (35.9) in the block group and 67.0 mg (32.4) in the sham block group. The mean difference in OME was&#x000a0;&#x02212;3.2 mg (95% confidence interval&#x000a0;&#x02212;15.3 to 8.1 mg, <italic>P</italic>=0.55).<fig id="fig2"><label>Fig 2</label><caption><p>Oral morphine equivalent consumption in 24 h for subjects receiving block or sham block. There were no significant differences between groups. CI, confidence interval.</p></caption><alt-text id="alttext0020">Fig 2</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr2"/></fig></p><p id="p0135">Secondary outcomes including NRS 0&#x02013;24 h (every 4 h) and postoperative opioid-related side-effects were similar between groups (<xref rid="tbl2" ref-type="table">Table&#x000a0;2</xref>). Quadriceps weakness as a result of FN block after fascia iliaca block can increase the risk of falls and delay mobilisation. Twenty-three participants (12 in the block group, 11 in the sham block group) were unable or required support to participate in physiotherapy on the first postoperative day primarily because of dizziness, nausea or pain. Operative limb weakness impeding physiotherapy on the first postoperative day was observed in four participants in the block group and three in the sham block group.<table-wrap position="float" id="tbl2"><label>Table&#x000a0;2</label><caption><p>Secondary outcomes. NRS, numeric rating scale for pain; NS, normal saline; POD; postoperative day; US-SIFIB, ultrasound-guided suprainguinal fascia iliaca compartment block.</p></caption><alt-text id="alttext0030">Table&#x000a0;2</alt-text><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th/><th>US-SIFIB(block<italic>n</italic>=66)</th><th>0.9% NS(shamblock<italic>n</italic>=67)</th><th><italic>P</italic>-value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>NRS rest</td><td/><td/><td>0.75</td></tr><tr><td>4 h</td><td>0.7 (1.2)</td><td>1.6 (2.2)</td><td/></tr><tr><td>8 h</td><td>1.7 (1.7)</td><td>2.1 (2.0)</td><td/></tr><tr><td>12 h</td><td>1.9 (1.9)</td><td>2.4 (1.9)</td><td/></tr><tr><td>16 h</td><td>1.8 (1.9)</td><td>2.1 (1.9)</td><td/></tr><tr><td>20 h</td><td>2.3 (2.1)</td><td>1.9 (1.5)</td><td/></tr><tr><td>24 h</td><td>1.9 (1.5)</td><td>1.8 (1.5)</td><td/></tr><tr><td>NRS movement</td><td/><td/><td>0.99</td></tr><tr><td>4 h</td><td>1.6 (2.2)</td><td>2.3 (2.6)</td><td/></tr><tr><td>8 h</td><td>2.9 (2.2)</td><td>3.5 (2.5)</td><td/></tr><tr><td>12 h</td><td>3.3 (2.3)</td><td>3.7 (2.2)</td><td/></tr><tr><td>16 h</td><td>2.8 (2.2)</td><td>3.7 (2.3)</td><td/></tr><tr><td>20 h</td><td>3.7 (2.3)</td><td>3.7 (2.3)</td><td/></tr><tr><td>24 h</td><td>3.9 (2.4)</td><td>4.4 (2.5)</td><td/></tr><tr><td colspan="4">Opioid-related side-effects (Y/N)</td></tr><tr><td>Respiratory depression</td><td>0/66</td><td>0/67</td><td>1.00</td></tr><tr><td>Nausea or vomiting</td><td>12/54</td><td>9/58</td><td>0.56</td></tr><tr><td>Pruritis</td><td>1/65</td><td>1/66</td><td>1.00</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4">POD 1 physiotherapy outcomes (Y/N)</td></tr><tr><td>Physiotherapy</td><td>54/12</td><td>56/11</td><td>0.79</td></tr><tr><td>Quadricep weakness</td><td>4/62</td><td>3/64</td><td>0.68</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></p></sec><sec id="sec3"><title>Discussion</title><p id="p0140">Effective pain management after THA not only promotes patient recovery and reduces postoperative complications, but also improves patient satisfaction. In this randomised trial, we studied the analgesic effect of US-SIFIB in patients undergoing primary THA under spinal anaesthesia. We failed to demonstrate a reduction in cumulative opioid consumption at 24 h. This contrasts with several studies that have shown a reduction in pain intensity in the early postoperative period (&#x0003c;24 h) and opioid-sparing effects of SIFIB when compared with placebo.</p><p id="p0145">Desmet and colleagues<xref rid="bib6" ref-type="bibr"><sup>6</sup></xref> conducted a randomised trial in 88 patients undergoing anterior approach THA under general anaesthesia and showed a 46% decrease in opioid consumption and lower pain scores at 24 h in those receiving SIFIB. The superior analgesic benefit was only in the first 4 h in the context of general anaesthesia. In our study, US-SIFICB was performed before the operation followed by administration of spinal anaesthesia. Although the duration of anaesthesia and analgesia after isobaric bupivacaine spinal is variable, it typically lasts 4&#x02013;6 h,<xref rid="bib9" ref-type="bibr"><sup>9</sup></xref> diminishing pain and opioid consumption. A single injection nerve block provides analgesia for 18&#x02013;24 h and a spinal anaesthetic overlaps up to 25% of the duration of the nerve block, obscuring some of the analgesic benefit in our study.</p><p id="p0150">Carella and colleagues<xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref> in a single blinded randomised controlled trial of 86 patients undergoing posterolateral THA with spinal anaesthesia (isobaric bupivacaine 0.5%, 2 ml, sufentanil 1 &#x003bc;g) reported significant reduction in 48-h cumulative morphine consumption and improved analgesia associated with SIFIB. The generalisability of the anaesthetic regimen is limited as the volume of local anaesthetic utilised in the spinal was less than commonly used, evident in the high conversion rate to general anaesthesia (14%) because of inadequate block. This would reduce the duration of sensory block, and hence analgesia from the spinal anaesthetic. Furthermore, lack of routine opioid-sparing analgesics (paracetamol, NSAID) would accentuate the opioid-sparing benefits of the nerve block and could account for the observed analgesic benefits. Gola and colleagues<xref rid="bib11" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref> demonstrated similar results in an open-label study of 109 patients undergoing posterolateral THA with low-dose spinal anaesthesia (hyperbaric bupivacaine 0.5%, 1.7&#x02013;2.2 ml).</p><p id="p0155">The analgesic effect of fascia iliaca block (not suprainguinal) has been challenged in several studies. Shariat and colleagues<xref rid="bib12" ref-type="bibr"><sup>12</sup></xref> failed to demonstrate a reduction in morphine consumption or pain intensity using a &#x02018;transverse&#x02019; approach fascia iliaca block (which effectively blocked the ON in only 25% of patients). Behrends and colleagues<xref rid="bib13" ref-type="bibr"><sup>13</sup></xref> in a double-blinded RCT indicated that preoperative fascia iliaca block did not improve analgesia after hip arthroscopy. Equivalent analgesic efficacy was reported by Gasanova and colleagues<xref rid="bib14" ref-type="bibr"><sup>14</sup></xref> for fascia iliaca block compared with periarticular infiltration for THA with a higher incidence of quadriceps weakness in the block group.</p><p id="p0160">In this study, in addition to consistent, standardised, multimodal analgesia with paracetamol and NSAID, all subjects were routinely prescribed a low dose of sustained-release hydromorphone every 8 h after operation as per our standard of practice. This baseline forced opioid consumption could have further diminished the difference in opioid consumption between study groups. This is another limitation, and indeed our institutional practice during the trial transitioned to only oral on-demand opioids.</p><p id="p0165">All participants underwent THA by the lateral approach. As the anterior approach becomes more frequently used, the generalisability of our study might be diminished. Indeed, early data show that opioid consumption is reduced when undergoing surgery by an anterior (63.1 mg OME) <italic>vs</italic> lateral (79.8 mg OME) approach.<xref rid="bib15" ref-type="bibr"><sup>15</sup></xref></p><p id="p0170">A major limitation of our study was that the success of US-SIFIB in participants was not assessed. Including participants with potential failed blocks (intention to treat analysis) could have reduced the opioid-sparing effect size observed in our trial. On balance, we accepted this limitation and favoured maintaining strict blinding to minimise bias in outcome assessment. However, all blocks were performed or supervised by skilled regional anaesthesiologists and the local anaesthetic volume used was consistent with recommendations for US-SIFIB.<xref rid="bib6" ref-type="bibr"><sup>6</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib16" ref-type="bibr"><sup>16</sup></xref> Block failure represents a small (in properly trained individuals) but inherent limitation of this regional technique for routine postoperative pain management for THA.</p><p id="p0175">Although US-SIFIB increases the risk of motor weakness, our data showed no difference in the ability to perform physiotherapy suggesting that this is of limited concern. Our postoperative pathway for THA during this study involved physiotherapy in the morning of postoperative day 1, which might have allowed for sufficient time for resolution of any motor weakness. This could be an issue in the context of ambulatory THA, however. Also, the ability to perform physiotherapy constituted a secondary variable and our trial might have been underpowered to detect significant differences in this outcome.</p><p id="p0180">In conclusion, addition of US-SIFIB with ropivacaine to a robust multimodal analgesic regimen was not superior to sham block in reducing opioid consumption. Additional investigation is required to elucidate the role of this block.</p></sec><sec id="sec4"><title>Authors&#x02019; contributions</title><p id="p0185">Study conception: BS, HT, AL, SC</p><p id="p0190">Study design: BS, PM, SC</p><p id="p0195">Study conduct: BS, HT, AL, PM, JG, LK, SC</p><p id="p0200">Data interpretation, drafting manuscript: BS, SC</p><p id="p0205">Approving final draft: all authors</p><p id="p0210">Data collection: HT, AL, LK</p><p id="p0215">Data analysis: AK</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="sec5"><title>Declaration of interest</title><p id="p0220">SC receives in-kind research support (no cost access to cognitive testing software) from CogState LLC, no relation to the work submitted. The other authors declare that they have no conflicts of interest.</p></sec><sec id="sec6"><title>Funding</title><p id="p0225">An unrestricted operating grant from the Sunnybrook Alternative Funding Plan Association/Innovation Fund of the Alternative Funding Plan from the <funding-source id="gs1">Academic Health Sciences Centres of Ontario</funding-source>.</p></sec><sec id="sec7"><title>Data sharing</title><p id="p0230">The full dataset is available from the corresponding authors upon request.</p></sec></body><back><ref-list id="cebib0010"><title>References</title><ref id="bib1"><label>1</label><element-citation publication-type="journal" id="sref1"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hojer Karlsen</surname><given-names>A.P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Geisler</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Petersen</surname><given-names>P.L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Mathiesen</surname><given-names>O.</given-names></name><name><surname>Dahl</surname><given-names>J.B.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Postoperative pain treatment after total hip arthroplasty: a systematic review</article-title><source>Pain</source><volume>156</volume><year>2015</year><fpage>8</fpage><lpage>30</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">25599296</pub-id>
